  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 311 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nMonday is trending not as wet compared to the weekend as the \nfrontal boundary sags south and stalls across IL/IN. Models do \nprog drier and cooler airmass to try and migrate into southern \nWI through the start of next week. However, given the frontal \nboundary placement along with globals models showing an upper-\nlevel trough swinging through the region, thinking there will be\noff and on light showers/drizzle through the start of the week \nnot too dissimilar to what we saw from the middle of this past \nweek. The main trough is expected to swing across the Midwest on\nTuesday with the the frontal placement being the main driver of\nrainfall amounts. Given the latest model trends, thinking it \nwill be more of light and dreary start of the week for southern \nWI as this activity will compete with a +1030mb high along and \njust north of the Great Lakes region. Still will need to monitor\nand see where the front stalls as a northern jog in the \nboundary or the surface high, could result in more moderate \nrainfall creeping back north into the area, but will continue to\nmonitor trends.\n\nOtherwise, upper-level ridge is progged to build across the Midwest \nalong with the surface high across the Great Lakes/eastern Canada \nthrough middle of next week. This will bring a drier airmass and \nnortheasterly flow to the area. However, may see more sunshine and a \nsoutherly shift in winds toward the end of the, which may bring a \nbit warmer temps to southern WI.\n\nWagner",
